What is the proper way to design and implement a greeting mechanism in Java that can take into account geographical coordinates, real-time hour, and language preferences while also considering changes in daylight saving schedules? Could you provide an example code snippet that incorporates all of these features?
Here is a sample JSON data that includes the relevant information:
{
  "location": {
    "latitude": "37.7749",
    "longitude": "-122.4194"
  },
  "time": "2022-03-18T16:42:01Z",
  "language": "en-US"
}
And here is an example code snippet in Java that uses this data to generate a customized greeting:
import java.util.Calendar;
import java.util.TimeZone;
public class Greeting {

  public static void main(String[] args) {

    String language = "en-US";
    double latitude = 37.7749;
    double longitude = -122.4194;
    String timeString = "2022-03-18T16:42:01Z";

    // Parse the time string into a Calendar object
    Calendar time = Calendar.getInstance(TimeZone.getTimeZone("UTC"));
    time.setTimeInMillis(javax.xml.bind.DatatypeConverter.parseDateTime(timeString).getTimeInMillis());

    // Determine the time of day based on the hour of the day
    String timeOfDay;
    int hour = time.get(Calendar.HOUR_OF_DAY);
    if (hour < 12) {
      timeOfDay = "morning";
    } else if (hour < 18) {
      timeOfDay = "afternoon";
    } else {
      timeOfDay = "evening";
    }

    // Determine the appropriate greeting based on the language and time of day
    String greeting;
    if (language.equals("en-US")) {
      if (latitude > 49.26) {
        greeting = "Good " + timeOfDay + ", eh?";
      } else if (latitude < 24.52) {
        greeting = "Howdy, y'all!";
      } else if (longitude < -124.39) {
        greeting = "Aloha!";
      } else if (longitude > -67.54) {
        greeting = "Good day!";
      } else {
        greeting = "Hi there!";
      }
    } else if (language.equals("es-ES")) {
      greeting = "¡Hola!";
    } else if (language.equals("fr-FR")) {
      greeting = "Bonjour!";
    } else {
      greeting = "Hello!";
    }

    System.out.println(greeting);

  }

}

